insert into public.moo_versions (id,document_id,label,body_md,change_note,status) values
('45180046-d1a1-574f-a9a4-92a1dcdc0fc6'::uuid,'11fd6dcf-233f-59ef-9607-d25f0953ebe4'::uuid,'Consolidated Draft 1.0',E'# Chapter 10. Institutional Sustainability, Deep Resilience and Recognition\n\n| **Chapter at a Glance** |                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n|-------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| What this chapter does  | Keeps the system working beyond one term or one consultant: the annual sustainability review, leadership succession, digital continuity, continuity checks, Gawad KALASAG and SGLG follow-up, innovation and the handover of the Manual and MODE. |\n| Main procedures         | SOP 10-01 to 10-07                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |\n| Read with               | Chapters 1 and 7                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| Status                  | Draft 1.0, 12 November 2026. Working draft for validation (workshop 1–4 December 2026); not approved.                                                                                                                                             |\n\n## 1. Introduction and Assessment-Informed Rationale\n\nChapters 1 to 9 set what the City does to reduce, prepare for, respond to and recover from disasters, how it pays for it, how it learns, whom it works with and how it builds capacity. This chapter asks a different question: how do these gains last? A good Manual can be shelved by the next administration; a trained team can leave; a platform can lapse when an account is not renewed; an award can be won one year and lost the next. Sustainability is the work of making the system outlast the people who built it.\n\nThe chapter has three parts. **Institutional sustainability** (sections 5 to 7) keeps the legal, human, financial and digital foundations in place. **Recognition** (section 8) treats Gawad KALASAG and the SGLG as independent evidence of progress, not as goals in themselves. **Deep resilience** (section 9) sets the path beyond compliance: fewer losses, faster recovery and adaptation to a changing climate, measured against the Sendai Framework and the Ten Essentials that the City''s own plan adopted.\n\nThe baseline shows where sustainability is weakest (Figure 10.1). Three of the gaps that carry the most weight in the whole assessment are about permanence: the legal basis of the CDRRMO as a regular office with its JMC 2014-1 divisions (A1.1, High), the filling of the minimum plantilla (A2.3, High) and the alternate EOC (B2.7, score 0, High). Continuity of public services (A3.4) and digital continuity and security (B3.3, B3.5) are still developing. The CDRRMO Head is permanent (A2.2), the EOC has its own budget line (B7.5), and the City takes part in recognition systems (A10.7), but the succession order, the readiness self-check (B7.6) and the follow-up of recognition gaps are not yet documented (Table 10.1).\n\n**Figure 10.1**\n\n*IAT v1.7 Scores for Sustainability and Recognition Indicators*\n\n![Figure 100: IAT v1.7 Scores for Sustainability and Recognition Indicators](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-100.webp)\n\n*Note. Scores copied from the A_CDRRMO and B_EOC sheets of IAT v1.7. Red outlines mark High gap priority: A1.1 and A2.3 ((4 − 2) × 3 × 1.5 = 9.0) and B2.7 ((4 − 0) × 2 × 1.5 = 12.0). All rows are pending validation at the workshop of 1–4 December 2026.*\n\n**Table 10.1**\n\n*Assessment Findings That Shape This Chapter*\n\n| **ID** | **Indicator (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                     | **Score** | **Priority** | **Finding and Owning Chapter**                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |\n|--------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------|--------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| B2.7   | Designated and equipped alternate EOC with relocation procedure (M, w2)                                      | 0         | High (12.0)  | No alternate EOC, relocation trigger or procedure. Owned by Chapter 2 (section 2.8); tested annually under SOP 10-04.                                                                                                                                                            |\n| A1.1   | Ordinance or EO creating the CDRRMO as a regular office with functions mirroring RA 10121 Sec. 12(c) (M, w3) | 2         | High (9.0)   | Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (amended by No. 011, s. 2015) creates the CDRRMO under the Office of the City Mayor with a Head and three LDRRMO I posts; the JMC 2014-1 divisions are not provided. Amendment proposed in Annex 1-A (Chapter 1). Score unchanged pending validation. |\n| A2.3   | Minimum JMC 2014-1 plantilla positions created and filled (M, w3)                                            | 2         | High (9.0)   | About 47 staff, four of them permanent, as reported at the FGD; CDP PPAs 1.1.1–1.1.2 plan the regular positions. Owned by Chapter 2.                                                                                                                                             |\n| A3.4   | Public Service Continuity Plan covering the CDRRMO and essential City services (M, w2)                       | 2         | Medium (6.0) | PSCP planned (CDP PPA 2.5); no PSCP, business impact analysis or essential-functions list. Owned by Chapter 3.                                                                                                                                                                   |\n| A2.2   | CDRRMO Head is a permanent plantilla position filled by a qualified appointee (M, w3)                        | 3         | Medium (4.5) | Permanent Head appointed; qualification records and the succession order are still to be documented (Chapter 2, section 11.2).                                                                                                                                                   |\n| B3.3   | Backup internet and continuity of digital systems (GP, w2)                                                   | 2         | Medium (4.0) | No second ISP, satellite link or failover test record. MODE works offline for routine tasks.                                                                                                                                                                                     |\n| B3.5   | Cybersecurity and information security for EOC systems (GP, w1)                                              | 1         | Low (3.0)    | Pending KII with the City ICT office; no EOC information-security policy.                                                                                                                                                                                                        |\n| B7.6   | Equipment maintenance schedule and annual EOC readiness self-check (GP, w1)                                  | 1         | Low (3.0)    | No preventive maintenance schedule or self-check using the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist.                                                                                                                                                                                  |\n| B7.5   | Dedicated EOC budget for operations, maintenance and upgrades in the LDRRMFIP (M, w2)                        | 3         | Low (3.0)    | The 10-Year Plan carries an OpCen programme line; utilisation reports to be validated. Owned by Chapter 6.                                                                                                                                                                       |\n| A10.7  | Participation in recognition systems (Gawad KALASAG, SGLG) and gaps acted upon (GP, w1)                      | 3         | Low (1.0)    | CDP goal: Gawad KALASAG yearly and 100% compliance with SGLG disaster indicators; action plans on remaining gaps not sighted.                                                                                                                                                    |\n\n*Note. Priority is the workbook band of (4 − score) × weight × 1.5 (Mandatory) or × 1.0 (Good Practice): High 9 or more, Medium 4 to 8.9, Low below 4. This chapter does not take over the items owned by other chapters; it checks each year that they stay in place (SOP 10-01). Annex 10-A gives the full row set.*\n\n### 1.1 What the Records Show\n\n- **A legal basis exists, but not the full structure.** Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (26 June 2014), amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015, creates the CDRRMO under the Office of the City Mayor with a Head (SG-23) and three LDRRMO I posts. Chapter 1 recasts the A1.1 finding as an amendment of that ordinance to provide the three JMC 2014-1 divisions and their staffing (Annex 1-A), and proposes an EO establishing the EOC (Annex 1-C).\n\n- **People.** The CDRRMO Head holds a permanent position. The FGD reported about 47 staff, of whom four are permanent; most are on job-order or casual terms. The CDP 2024–2030 programmes the creation of regular positions (PPAs 1.1.1–1.1.2) and the 10-Year Plan lists 4 + 4 + 2 plantilla items. SP Res. No. 2023-347 records the CDRRMO''s lack of response manpower.\n\n- **Recognition goals.** The CDP sets the goal of achieving the Gawad KALASAG award every year and full compliance with the SGLG disaster preparedness indicators. The 10-Year Plan cites the Gawad KALASAG Seal and the SGLG and observes that the City''s performance has been assessed mainly through such national systems.\n\n- **Networks for deeper resilience.** The City has an MCR2030 commitment, the NRC Resilient LGU partnership (SP Res. No. 2021-036) and the EDC Adopt-a-City programme, and is the model LGU of the DENR–EDC Project TRANSFORM (EO 2023-0026). The 10-Year Plan bases its monitoring on the UNDRR Ten Essentials.\n\n- **The platform.** MODE runs in a Supabase project under an organisation named for the City Government of Ormoc, in Tokyo (ap-northeast-1), and on a Vercel team, both on free tiers during development (ADR-026). The architecture decisions require City ownership, time-boxed developer access removed at turnover, portability to any Postgres host, and a decision by the City on paid, City-owned hosting with backups before MODE is used for real operations. Weekly backup and quarterly restore runbooks exist in the repository.\n\n### 1.2 Design Considerations\n\n1.  **Institutions, not individuals.** Every critical role has an alternate and a succession order; every critical document has an owner office and a version; every agreement names positions.\n\n2.  **Law before budget, budget before people.** An office that exists in ordinance and has a line in the budget survives changes of leadership better than one that exists by practice.\n\n3.  **Continuity is tested, not assumed.** The alternate EOC, the PSCP, the backups and the offline mode are proven once a year.\n\n4.  **Recognition is evidence.** Awards confirm progress measured independently; chasing scores for their own sake drifts toward paperwork.\n\n5.  **The City owns its system.** Data, code, accounts and keys belong to the City and can be run by a local IT contractor without the developer.\n\n6.  **Beyond compliance.** Compliance is the floor. The aim is fewer deaths, smaller losses and faster recovery, measured over years.\n\n## 2. Purpose and Intended Results\n\nThis chapter sets how the City keeps its DRRM institutions, people, funds, systems and knowledge in place over time; how it uses recognition systems; and how it plans its progress from compliance toward deeper resilience.\n\nIntended results:\n\n1.  The CDRRMO has a full legal basis with its JMC 2014-1 divisions, and the EOC is established by issuance (A1.1, B1.1).\n\n2.  Every critical position has an alternate and a documented succession order, and leadership transitions follow a handover protocol (A2.2).\n\n3.  The PSCP, the alternate EOC and the EOC readiness self-check are tested every year (A3.4, B2.7, B7.6).\n\n4.  MODE runs on City-owned, paid hosting with backups, restore tests, an access review and an information-security policy before it is used for real operations (B3.3, B3.5; MR-10).\n\n5.  The City takes part in the Gawad KALASAG Seal and the SGLG each year, and every gap found is entered in the CAPA register and acted on (A10.7).\n\n6.  A sustainability review each year checks that the legal, human, financial, digital and knowledge foundations are still in place, and updates the resilience roadmap.\n\n7.  At the end of the engagement, the Manual and MODE are handed over to City custody with no dependence on the developer.\n\n## 3. Scope, Application and Institutional Boundaries\n\n**In scope:** the annual sustainability review; legal permanence of the CDRRMO and EOC; leadership succession and transition; financial sustainability lines; continuity testing (PSCP, alternate EOC, readiness self-check); digital continuity and information security of MODE and EOC systems; recognition systems; the resilience roadmap and innovation pipeline; stewardship and handover of the Manual and MODE.\n\n**Table 10.2**\n\n*Subjects Owned by Other Chapters and Checked Here*\n\n| **Subject**                                                                          | **Owning Chapter** |\n|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|--------------------|\n| Ordinance amendment (Annex 1-A) and EOC establishment EO (Annex 1-C)                 | Chapter 1          |\n| Plantilla, positions, succession order, EOC facility and alternate EOC (section 2.8) | Chapter 2          |\n| Public Service Continuity Plan                                                       | Chapter 3          |\n| LDRRMF lines for the EOC, maintenance and replacement                                | Chapter 6          |\n| IAT cycle, CAPA register, records and data protection                                | Chapter 7          |\n| Partnerships and networks                                                            | Chapter 8          |\n| Capacity development and refresher cycles                                            | Chapter 9          |\n\n**Institutional boundaries.** The SP enacts ordinances; the Mayor issues executive orders, designates officers-in-charge and decides on hosting and contracts; the HRMO and the Civil Service rules govern appointments and designations; the City ICT office and the DPO govern information systems and personal data; OCD and the DILG decide recognition results. The CDRRMO prepares, tracks and reports.\n\n## 4. Legal, Policy and Operational Framework\n\n**Table 10.3**\n\n*Instruments Governing Sustainability and Recognition*\n\n| **Instrument**                                                                     | **What It Requires or Allows**                                                                                    | **How This Chapter Applies It**               |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------|\n| RA 10121 Secs. 11 and 12                                                           | The LDRRMC and a permanent LDRRMO with defined functions.                                                         | Section 5; SOP 10-01                          |\n| NDRRMC–DBM–DILG–CSC JMC No. 2014-1                                                 | Organisational structure and staffing of LDRRMOs.                                                                 | Section 5; SOP 10-01                          |\n| Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended by No. 011, s. 2015                         | Creates the CDRRMO under the Office of the City Mayor.                                                            | Section 5 (amendment in Chapter 1, Annex 1-A) |\n| RA 7160 (Local Government Code)                                                    | Legislative and executive powers; designation of officers; local budgets.                                         | Sections 5, 6                                 |\n| Civil Service rules on designation and officers-in-charge                          | Who may act for a head of office and for how long.                                                                | SOP 10-02; Chapter 2                          |\n| NDRRMC Memo No. 131, s. 2018                                                       | Establishment, operation and management of the EOC, including an alternate EOC.                                   | SOP 10-04                                     |\n| NDRRMC–OCD Public Service Continuity Planning Guidebook                            | Essential functions, business impact analysis, alternate sites, orders of succession, vital records.              | SOP 10-04                                     |\n| EOC Capability Assessment Checklist (OCD, 2017)                                    | Readiness criteria for primary and alternate EOCs.                                                                | SOP 10-04 (B7.6)                              |\n| RA 10173 (Data Privacy Act); NPC Circular No. 2023-06; NPC Circular No. 16-03      | Security of personal data; breach management.                                                                     | Section 7; SOP 10-03                          |\n| RA 9470 (National Archives of the Philippines Act)                                 | Records retention and disposal.                                                                                   | Section 7; SOP 10-07                          |\n| NDRRMC MC No. 03, s. 2025; OCD Memo No. 763, s. 2026                               | Gawad KALASAG Seal for LDRRMCOs: minimum requirements, criteria and weights, tiers, process and CY 2026 timeline. | Section 8; SOP 10-05; Annex 10-B              |\n| RA 11292 (Seal of Good Local Governance Act)                                       | SGLG assessment, including disaster preparedness; criteria set each year by DILG.                                 | Section 8; SOP 10-05                          |\n| Sendai Framework 2015–2030; UNDRR Ten Essentials and Disaster Resilience Scorecard | Global targets; city resilience benchmark adopted as the base of the City''s M&E.                                  | Section 9; MR-18                              |\n| RA 9729 as amended by RA 10174; LCCAP 2019–2030                                    | Climate change adaptation planning.                                                                               | Section 9                                     |\n\n## 5. Institutional Sustainability\n\nFive foundations keep the City''s DRRM system standing (Figure 10.2). If any one is missing, the others carry more weight than they can bear: a well-funded office without legal permanence can be dissolved; a well-written Manual without trained people is not used; a platform without backups can lose years of records in a day.\n\n**Figure 10.2**\n\n*Foundations of Sustainable DRRM in Ormoc*\n\n![Figure 101: Foundations of Sustainable DRRM in Ormoc](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-101.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter. The foundation (approved Manual, IAT cycle and CAPA register) is set in Chapters 1 and 7; the five pillars are checked each year in the sustainability review (SOP 10-01).*\n\n### 5.1 Legal and Institutional Permanence\n\nThe CDRRMO already exists in ordinance. Permanence now means amending Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 so that it provides the JMC 2014-1 divisions (Administration and Training, Research and Planning, Operations and Warning) and their functions and staffing; establishing the EOC by issuance with its levels, activation authority and roster; keeping the CDRRMC constituted with all its seats; and keeping the Manual approved and versioned. The sustainability review tracks each instrument (SUS-F01): drafted, endorsed, enacted, implemented.\n\n### 5.2 People and Succession\n\nThe Head, the division chiefs, the EOC Manager and the duty officers each have a named alternate and a written order of succession (Chapter 2, section 11.2). When a holder leaves, is on leave or is unavailable in an emergency, the alternate acts without waiting. After each local election, or when the CDRRMO Head changes, a transition protocol (SOP 10-02) briefs the incoming Mayor and CDRRMC on the City''s risks, the Manual, open CAPA items, the budget lines and any declaration in force, and confirms the designations that the new administration must reissue. Critical knowledge held by one person is written into the Manual, a protocol or the Vault.\n\n### 5.3 Financial Sustainability\n\nRecurring costs that keep the system working are budgeted as lines, not found event by event: EOC operations and maintenance (B7.5), equipment preventive maintenance and replacement (A8.3, B7.6), stock replenishment (Chapter 6), training and exercises (Chapter 9), insurance (A4.7), and the hosting and support of MODE once the City adopts it for real operations. The sustainability review checks that each line is in the LDRRMFIP and in use (Chapter 6, FIN-F03).\n\n## 6. Continuity of Operations\n\nThe City must keep its essential DRRM functions running when the EOC building, its power, its network or its people are affected by the same disaster. The PSCP (Chapter 3) names the essential functions and how they continue; the alternate EOC (Chapter 2, section 2.8) gives a second site; the EOC readiness self-check (B7.6) confirms both are ready. This chapter sets one annual continuity check (SOP 10-04) that brings the three together: a relocation drill to the alternate EOC, a PSCP exercise for the CDRRMO''s essential functions, and the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist for both sites. Findings go to the CAPA register.\n\n## 7. Digital Continuity and Information Security\n\nMODE now holds the Manual, the assessment, the protocols, the logbook, the Vault and the EOC''s operating records. Its continuity is part of the City''s continuity. The architecture already favours it: the data are plain Postgres and SQL migrations that can move to any Postgres host; the app works offline for routine tasks and keeps authority actions online-only; the City owns the project; the developer''s access is time-boxed. What remains is to put the operating controls in place and test them (Figure 10.3).\n\n**Figure 10.3**\n\n*Digital Continuity Cycle for MODE*\n\n![Figure 102: Digital Continuity Cycle for MODE](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-102.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter from ADR-002, ADR-021 and ADR-026, DEPLOY.md sections 8 and 11, and docs/runbook-backup-restore.md in the MODE repository (version 1.16.0).*\n\n**Table 10.4**\n\n*Digital Continuity and Security Controls*\n\n| **Control**                 | **Requirement**                                                                                                                                                                                                 | **Owner**                           |\n|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------|\n| Hosting decision            | Before MODE is used for real operations or holds personal data, the City moves from the development free tiers to City-owned, paid hosting with daily backups, and records the decision in a new ADR (ADR-026). | City Mayor; City ICT office         |\n| Backups                     | Daily database backups (hosting plan); weekly pg_dump and copy of the evidence and Vault buckets, which the hosting backups do not cover.                                                                       | City ICT office; platform custodian |\n| Restore tests               | Quarterly restore into the separate training project, with the runbook checklist; before go-live.                                                                                                               | City ICT office                     |\n| Access                      | Named accounts; role-based access in the database; multi-factor authentication for administrators; quarterly access review; shared-device sign-out (ADR-004, ADR-014).                                          | City ICT office; CDRRMO Head        |\n| Developer access            | Time-boxed; removed at turnover with the admin script, and confirmed in the handover record.                                                                                                                    | CDRRMO Head; City ICT office        |\n| Connectivity                | A second internet path (another ISP or a satellite terminal) for the EOC, tested quarterly; offline copies of forms and contact lists (B3.3).                                                                   | City ICT office                     |\n| Information-security policy | An EOC information-security policy covering accounts, devices, backups, incident reporting to the DPO and the NPC rules (B3.5).                                                                                 | City ICT office; DPO                |\n| Data location               | The privacy notice and the DPO''s assessment cover the transfer to Japan (Tokyo region) (ADR-021).                                                                                                               | DPO                                 |\n| Records                     | Vault documents superseded, never deleted; retention and disposal per RA 9470 schedules.                                                                                                                        | City Records Officer                |\n| Maintainability             | Plain SQL, common TypeScript and documented runbooks so that a local IT contractor can maintain the platform; an ICT asset register.                                                                            | City ICT office                     |\n\n> VALIDATION NOTE. The hosting arrangement described here is the development arrangement recorded in ADR-026 (11 November 2026). The City''s decision on production hosting, and the DPO''s assessment of the Japan transfer, are prerequisites for operational use and are listed in section 16.\n\n## 8. Recognition Systems\n\n### 8.1 Gawad KALASAG Seal for LDRRMC’s, CY 2026\n\nThe Gawad KALASAG Seal for Local DRRM Councils and Offices is governed by NDRRMC MC No. 03, s. 2025 (11 April 2025), and its CY 2026 conduct by OCD Memo No. 763, s. 2026. All 1,724 provinces, cities and municipalities are assessed. The LGU self-assesses with the prescribed LDRRMCO Assessment Tool, attaches its means of verification and a notarised Omnibus Sworn Statement, and submits to OCD Region VIII, whose Regional Validation Committee runs a desk review and may validate in the field. Results for CY 2026 are due not later than 30 November 2026. Special Awards are suspended for CY 2026 pending a supplemental guideline.\n\nAn LGU that fails any of six minimum requirements is acknowledged for participation but cannot be recognised. Table 10.5 sets them against the City''s records. This is the CDRRMO''s own reading for planning; only OCD Region VIII rates the City.\n\n**Table 10.5**\n\n*Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 Minimum Requirements and the City''s Records*\n\n| **No.** | **Minimum Requirement (OCD Memo No. 763, S. 2026)**                                                                               | **City Record (for Validation)**                                                                                | **Related IAT and Chapter** |\n|---------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|\n| a       | Established and organised LDRRMC                                                                                                  | EO 2024-032A reorganises the CDRRMC; it meets at least quarterly.                                               | A1.2, A1.3; Chapter 1       |\n| b       | Membership of non-state stakeholders in the LDRRMC (4 CSOs and 1 private sector)                                                  | Five CSO and two private-sector seats in EO 2024-032A; SP accreditation of the CSOs not shown.                  | A1.2; Chapter 8 (SOP 8-06)  |\n| c       | Established LDRRM Office                                                                                                          | Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 as amended by No. 011, s. 2015.                                                      | A1.1; Chapter 1             |\n| d       | Permanent and functional LDRRM Officer compliant with JMC 2014-1                                                                  | Permanent Head appointed; qualification records and JMC 2014-1 staffing to be confirmed.                        | A2.2, A2.3; Chapter 2       |\n| e       | Established and functional Operations Center                                                                                      | 24-hour OpCen duty observed in October 2026; roster and handover records to be attached.                        | B4.3; Chapters 2 and 4      |\n| f       | Approved and adopted LDRRMP, reviewed by the reviewing authority, covering the assessment year and consistent with the NDRRM Plan | DRRM 10-Year Plan 2023–2032 adopted by SP Res. No. 2023-112; review by the reviewing authority to be confirmed. | A3.1; Chapter 3             |\n\n*Note. Requirements quoted from OCD Memo No. 763, s. 2026, p. 4. The "City record" column is a planning reading for validation, not a rating.*\n\nAbove the minimum, the LGU is scored on six criteria with 39 indicators in all (Figure 10.4). The weighted average sets the recognition: Gold at 2.50 and above, Silver at 2.00 to 2.49, Bronze at 1.50 to 1.99.\n\n**Figure 10.4**\n\n*Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 Criteria, Number of Indicators and Weights*\n\n![Figure 103: Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 Criteria, Number of Indicators and Weights](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-103.webp)\n\n*Note. From OCD Memo No. 763, s. 2026, section 5.1 (applying NDRRMC MC No. 03, s. 2025) and section 6 (recognition tiers).*\n\n### 8.2 Seal of Good Local Governance\n\nThe SGLG under RA 11292 includes disaster preparedness among its assessment areas. Its criteria are set each year by the DILG and change from year to year, so this Manual does not fix them. Each year the CDRRMO obtains the current criteria from the DILG City Office, maps them to the IAT and the Manual, and prepares the evidence through the Vault (SOP 10-05).\n\n### 8.3 Using Recognition as Evidence\n\nThe IAT and the recognition systems measure overlapping things in different ways. Annex 10-B maps the Gawad KALASAG criteria to IAT domains so that one body of evidence in the Vault serves both. Results and assessor comments are entered in the CAPA register like any other finding. The City does not create documents only to satisfy an assessor; every document filed as evidence must also be in use. The MODE Gawad KALASAG self-check tool is to be updated to the CY 2026 tiers before it is used (Coherence Analysis, open issue II.2-2).\n\n## 9. Deep Resilience: Beyond Compliance\n\nCompliance with the IAT Mandatory items and the Gawad KALASAG minimum requirements is the floor. Deep resilience is measured by outcomes: fewer deaths and missing persons, fewer people affected, smaller economic losses, less disruption of basic services, and faster recovery (Sendai Targets A to D), and by the City''s position on the UNDRR Ten Essentials, which the 10-Year Plan adopted as the base of its monitoring.\n\n### 9.1 The Resilience Roadmap\n\n**Figure 10.5 proposes three horizons aligned with the LDRRMP 2023–2032 and the Sendai Framework. The first institutionalises what the Manual sets out; the second consolidates it through practice and the plan''s mid-term evaluation; the third deepens it toward Sendai 2030 and the next LDRRMP. Within each horizon the order of work follows this priority order: legal constitution first, then the operating systems that carry whole domains, then high-leverage compliance items. Targets for the IMI, Mandatory compliance and the Interface Index are set by the CDRRMC; this chapter sets none.**\n\n**Figure 10.5**\n\n*Proposed Resilience Roadmap, 2026–2032*\n\n![Figure 104: 9.1 The Resilience Roadmap](/library/moo-consolidated/fig-104.webp)\n\n*Note. Prepared for this chapter. Horizons and their contents are proposals for CDRRMC decision; they follow the LDRRMP period (2023–2032), its mid-term report (2029) and the Sendai Framework (2015–2030).*\n\n### 9.2 Deepening Areas\n\n- **Anticipatory action maturity.** From the first funded AA line and the first declaration under RA 12287 toward impact-based triggers, pre-agreed actions by sector and evaluated results (Chapters 4, 6 and 7).\n\n- **Climate adaptation and nature-based solutions.** Linking the LDRRMP with the LCCAP 2019–2030 and Project TRANSFORM: watershed protection in the Anilao–Malbasag catchment, mangrove restoration and climate-smart agriculture (Chapter 8, Part C).\n\n- **Risk-informed development.** Bringing CDRA scenarios into the CLUP and CDP and screening investments (Part C, C1), so that new risk is not created.\n\n- **Social protection and inclusion.** Connecting relief and recovery with social protection and livelihood programmes so that the poorest recover first (Chapter 5).\n\n- **Learning city.** Peer learning through MCR2030 and the NRC, and contributing Ormoc''s own lessons to other LGUs (Chapter 7).\n\n### 9.3 Innovation Pipeline\n\nNew ideas (a sensor network, a community feedback tool, a new financing instrument) are tried as pilots with a stated purpose, a measure of success, a time limit and a data-protection check; evaluated; and then adopted into the Manual, scaled or dropped (SOP 10-06). A pilot is not a programme until it has been evaluated. Adopted innovations are shared with peers through Chapter 7.\n\n## 10. Stewardship and Handover of the Manual and MODE\n\nThe Manual and MODE were developed with the City under a fourteen-week engagement. Their value lasts only if the City can keep, revise and run them alone. At the end of the engagement, and again whenever a custodian changes, the stewardship protocol (SOP 10-07) confirms that the City holds the approved Manual and its source files, the Library versions, the repository, the hosting accounts and keys, the runbooks, the training materials and the handover record; that the developer''s access has been removed; and that a named City custodian and a local IT contractor can maintain the platform. The Manual is revised under Chapter 7 (SOP 7-08) and approved only by the CDRRMO Head.\n\n## 11. Standard Operating Procedures\n\nEach SOP has a control block and a step table. Every step states the actor (a position or office, never a person), the action and decision rule, inputs and outputs, and timing, control and escalation. Form codes are those of section 15. All MODE templates named here are proposals; none of SOPs 10-01 to 10-07 is yet loaded in MODE.\n\n### SOP 10-01 \\| Annual Sustainability Review and Resilience Roadmap Update\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning. **Purpose:** Check each year that the legal, human, financial, digital and knowledge foundations are in place, and update the roadmap.\n\n**Table 10.6**\n\n**SOP 10-01 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                         |\n|-----------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-01 (proposed)                                                                  |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMC receives the review and decides on the roadmap; SP and Mayor for instruments               |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                      |\n| Trigger                     | First quarter of each year, after the IAT cycle closes and the annual results report is presented |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F01; SUS-F02                                                                                  |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A1.1 (2, High); A2.3 (2, High); B7.5 (3, Low); all IAT results                                    |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-01; MR-02; MR-11                                                                               |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121 Secs. 11–12; JMC 2014-1; Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 as amended                           |\n| Cross-references            | Chapters 1, 2, 6 and 7; SOP 10-05                                                                 |\n\n**Table 10.7**\n\n**SOP 10-01 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Track instruments</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Record the status of each legal instrument: ordinance amendment, EOC EO, CDRRMC EO, Manual version, agreements.</p>\n<p>Decision: Any instrument stalled for more than a year?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Vault; Chapter 1 register</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F01 tracker</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: January</p>\n<p>Control: Status sourced to documents</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Check the pillars</th>\n<th>Research and Planning with division chiefs</th>\n<th><p>Check people (plantilla, alternates, succession), finance (lines in use), digital (SOP 10-03 results), knowledge (Manual, lessons, register).</p>\n<p>Decision: Each pillar has evidence?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Chapter records</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F02 checklist</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: February</p>\n<p>Control: Evidence linked</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Read the results</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Compare IAT results, the Interface Index, recognition results and the annual results report with last year.</p>\n<p>Decision: Progress or slippage?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: IAT; ME-F02; recognition results</p>\n<p>Out: Analysis</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: February</p>\n<p>Control: Same instruments as last year</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Update the roadmap</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Propose next year''s priorities by horizon and Pareto tier; propose targets for CDRRMC decision.</p>\n<p>Decision: Priorities funded or fundable?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Analysis</p>\n<p>Out: Roadmap update</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: March</p>\n<p>Control: Linked to the LDRRMFIP call</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Decide and record</th>\n<th>CDRRMC</th>\n<th><p>Approve the roadmap update and targets; enter gaps in the CAPA register.</p>\n<p>Decision: Approved?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Roadmap update</p>\n<p>Out: CDRRMC resolution; ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: First quarter meeting</p>\n<p>Control: Resolution filed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC Chair</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** SUS-F01 tracker; SUS-F02 checklist; analysis; roadmap update; CDRRMC resolution; CAPA entries.\n\n### SOP 10-02 \\| Leadership Transition and Succession\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head (or the officer-in-charge), with the City Administrator and the HRMO. **Purpose:** Keep DRRM functions continuous through elections, changes of the CDRRMO Head and absences of key officers.\n\n**Table 10.8**\n\n**SOP 10-02 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                                                           |\n|-----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Protocol: PR-LEADERSHIP-TRANSITION (proposed)                                                                                       |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | City Mayor designates officers-in-charge and reissues designations under Civil Service rules                                        |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Head; City Administrator                                                                                                     |\n| Trigger                     | After each local election; appointment of a new CDRRMO Head; absence of a key officer beyond the period set in the succession order |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F03; Annex 10-C                                                                                                                 |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A2.2 (3, Medium); B4.2 (Chapter 2)                                                                                                  |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-02; MR-09                                                                                                                        |\n| Policy basis                | RA 7160; Civil Service rules on designation; RA 10121 Sec. 12                                                                       |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 2 (section 11.2; HR-05, HR-06); Chapter 1 (CDRRMC)                                                                          |\n\n**Table 10.9**\n\n**SOP 10-02 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Keep the succession order current</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with HRMO</th>\n<th><p>Keep a written order of succession and alternates for the Head, division chiefs, EOC Manager and duty officers.</p>\n<p>Decision: Every critical role has an alternate?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Chapter 2 roster</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F03 succession order</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Reviewed each January</p>\n<p>Control: Approved by the City Administrator</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Prepare the briefing pack</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Prepare the transition pack (Annex 10-C): risks, Manual, open CAPA items, budget lines, declarations in force, agreements, designations to reissue.</p>\n<p>Decision: Pack complete?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Registers</p>\n<p>Out: Transition pack</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before an election or a handover</p>\n<p>Control: Pack dated</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Act in the interim</th>\n<th>Designated alternate</th>\n<th><p>On absence or vacancy, act under the succession order; inform the EOC roster.</p>\n<p>Decision: Authority to act confirmed in writing?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: SUS-F03</p>\n<p>Out: Designation or office order</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Immediately</p>\n<p>Control: No gap in duty coverage</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Brief the new leadership</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head or OIC</th>\n<th><p>Brief the incoming Mayor, CDRRMC and Head; walk through the Manual and MODE roles.</p>\n<p>Decision: Briefing held?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Transition pack</p>\n<p>Out: Briefing record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days of assumption</p>\n<p>Control: Attendance recorded</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Reissue designations</th>\n<th>City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Reissue EOC, CDRRMC and focal designations as needed; update MODE roles.</p>\n<p>Decision: All designations current?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Designation list</p>\n<p>Out: Issuances; MODE role changes</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 60 days</p>\n<p>Control: Old accounts removed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** SUS-F03 succession order; transition packs; designations; briefing records; MODE role changes.\n\n### SOP 10-03 \\| Digital Continuity: Backup, Restore Test, Access Review and Connectivity\n**Process owner:** City ICT office, with the MODE platform custodian and the CDRRMO Head. **Purpose:** Make sure MODE and the EOC''s digital systems can be recovered and stay secure.\n\n**Table 10.10**\n\n**SOP 10-03 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                    |\n|-----------------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-03 (proposed); repository runbooks                                        |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | City Mayor decides on hosting and contracts; DPO for personal data                           |\n| Process owner (operational) | City ICT office                                                                              |\n| Trigger                     | Weekly, quarterly and annual schedule; a security incident; a change of hosting or custodian |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F04; Annex 10-D                                                                          |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | B3.3 (2, Medium); B3.5 (1, Low)                                                              |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-10; MR-06; MR-15                                                                          |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10173; NPC Circulars No. 2023-06 and No. 16-03; ADR-002, ADR-021, ADR-026                 |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 7 (SOP 7-09); DEPLOY.md sections 8 and 11; docs/runbook-backup-restore.md            |\n\n**Table 10.11**\n\n**SOP 10-03 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Decide production hosting</th>\n<th>City Mayor with City ICT office and DPO</th>\n<th><p>Before operational use, decide City-owned, paid hosting with daily backups; record a new ADR; confirm the DPO''s assessment of the data location.</p>\n<p>Decision: Decision made before operational use?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: ADR-026</p>\n<p>Out: Decision record; new ADR</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before go-live</p>\n<p>Control: Reviewed by the City Legal Office</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Weekly backup</th>\n<th>City ICT office</th>\n<th><p>Check the daily backup; take a pg_dump; copy the evidence and Vault buckets; clear old error reports.</p>\n<p>Decision: Backup present and readable?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Runbook</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F04 weekly entries</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Weekly</p>\n<p>Control: Copies stored off the hosting provider</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Restore test</th>\n<th>City ICT office</th>\n<th><p>Restore into the training project and run the checklist.</p>\n<p>Decision: Restored data complete and the app works?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Backups</p>\n<p>Out: Restore test record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Quarterly and before go-live</p>\n<p>Control: Second person checks</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Access review</th>\n<th>City ICT office with CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Review accounts and roles; remove leavers; confirm MFA for administrators.</p>\n<p>Decision: Any account without a current owner or role?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Account list</p>\n<p>Out: Access review record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Quarterly</p>\n<p>Control: Changes logged</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Connectivity test</th>\n<th>City ICT office with EOC</th>\n<th><p>Switch to the second internet path; run the EOC in offline mode for a set period.</p>\n<p>Decision: EOC kept working?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Network setup</p>\n<p>Out: Test record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Quarterly (connectivity); annually (offline)</p>\n<p>Control: Results to the CAPA register</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>6. Incident response</th>\n<th>City ICT office with DPO</th>\n<th><p>On a security incident, contain, assess and, where personal data are involved, follow Chapter 7 (SOP 7-09).</p>\n<p>Decision: Personal data affected?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Incident</p>\n<p>Out: Incident record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Immediately</p>\n<p>Control: NPC 72-hour rule where it applies</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Hosting decision and ADR; SUS-F04 backup log; restore test records; access reviews; connectivity and offline test records; incident records.\n\n### SOP 10-04 \\| Annual Continuity Check: Alternate EOC Relocation, PSCP Exercise and EOC Readiness Self-Check\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with the EOC Manager. **Purpose:** Prove once a year that essential DRRM functions continue if the EOC, its power, its network or its people are lost.\n\n**Table 10.12**\n\n**SOP 10-04 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                      |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-04 (proposed)                                                               |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head; Mayor for activation of the alternate EOC in a real event                         |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Head; EOC Manager                                                                       |\n| Trigger                     | Annual schedule, before the typhoon season; a change of EOC site; an AAR finding on continuity |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F05; EOC Capability Assessment Checklist                                                   |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | B2.7 (0, High); A3.4 (2, Medium); B7.6 (1, Low)                                                |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-10; MR-04; MR-11; MR-03                                                                     |\n| Policy basis                | NDRRMC Memo No. 131, s. 2018; PSCP Guidebook; EOC Capability Assessment Checklist (OCD, 2017)  |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 2 (section 2.8); Chapter 3 (PSCP); Chapter 9 (SOP 9-03); Chapter 7 (SOP 7-04)          |\n\n**Table 10.13**\n\n**SOP 10-04 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Self-check both sites</th>\n<th>EOC Manager</th>\n<th><p>Complete the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist for the primary and alternate EOC: site, building, power, communications, supplies, security.</p>\n<p>Decision: Any critical item missing?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Checklist</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F05 self-check</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before the season</p>\n<p>Control: Evidence photographed and filed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Relocation drill</th>\n<th>EOC Manager</th>\n<th><p>Relocate a working EOC cell to the alternate site with the go-kit; run a SitRep cycle from there.</p>\n<p>Decision: Operational within the target time set in Chapter 2?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Go-kit; relocation procedure</p>\n<p>Out: Drill record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Timed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. PSCP exercise</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with PSCP team</th>\n<th><p>Exercise the CDRRMO''s essential functions under a loss scenario (site, power, staff).</p>\n<p>Decision: Essential functions continued?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: PSCP</p>\n<p>Out: Exercise record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Evaluated under Chapter 7</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>3a. Maintenance check</th>\n<th>EOC Logistics</th>\n<th><p>Confirm the preventive maintenance schedule for EOC equipment was followed.</p>\n<p>Decision: Overdue maintenance?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Maintenance log</p>\n<p>Out: Maintenance status</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Annually</p>\n<p>Control: Log signed</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>4. Follow up</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Enter gaps in the CAPA register; report to the sustainability review.</p>\n<p>Decision: Each gap owned?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Records</p>\n<p>Out: ME-F05 entries</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 15 days</p>\n<p>Control: Tracked to closure</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** SUS-F05 self-checks; relocation drill record; PSCP exercise record; maintenance status; CAPA entries.\n\n### SOP 10-05 \\| Recognition Systems: Gawad KALASAG and SGLG Participation and Gap Follow-Up\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Research and Planning. **Purpose:** Take part each year with complete, truthful evidence, and turn every gap found into an improvement.\n\n**Table 10.14**\n\n**SOP 10-05 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                             |\n|-----------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-05 (proposed); MODE Gawad KALASAG self-check tool (to be updated to CY 2026 tiers) |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | City Mayor signs submissions and the Omnibus Sworn Statement; OCD Region VIII and DILG decide results |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                          |\n| Trigger                     | OCD and DILG calls for submission; release of results                                                 |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F06; Annex 10-B                                                                                   |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A10.7 (3, Low)                                                                                        |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-07; MR-11                                                                                          |\n| Policy basis                | NDRRMC MC No. 03, s. 2025; OCD Memo No. 763, s. 2026; RA 11292                                        |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 7 (Vault, CAPA); SOP 10-01                                                                    |\n\n**Table 10.15**\n\n**SOP 10-05 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Obtain the current rules</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Obtain the current Gawad KALASAG guidelines and assessment tool from OCD VIII and the SGLG criteria from the DILG City Office.</p>\n<p>Decision: Current year''s version?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Issuances</p>\n<p>Out: Rules on file</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: On the call</p>\n<p>Control: Vault entries</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Check minimum requirements</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Check the six Gawad KALASAG minimum requirements (Table 10.5) and the SGLG core requirements.</p>\n<p>Decision: Any minimum not met? Fix before submission or report to the Mayor.</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Rules; records</p>\n<p>Out: Minimum-requirement check</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: On the call</p>\n<p>Control: Evidence linked</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Self-assess</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>Complete the self-assessment honestly with the prescribed tool; use the MODE self-check only as a guide.</p>\n<p>Decision: Every rating backed by a document in use?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Tool; Vault</p>\n<p>Out: Self-assessment; SUS-F06</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Per OCD VIII timeline</p>\n<p>Control: Second person reviews</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Submit</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head; City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Compile means of verification; the Mayor signs; submit to OCD VIII and DILG as required.</p>\n<p>Decision: Complete and on time?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Self-assessment</p>\n<p>Out: Submission; receipt</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: By the deadline</p>\n<p>Control: Notarised Omnibus Sworn Statement attached</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Follow up</th>\n<th>Research and Planning</th>\n<th><p>On results, enter every gap and assessor comment in the CAPA register; report to the CDRRMC.</p>\n<p>Decision: All gaps entered?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Results</p>\n<p>Out: ME-F05 entries; report</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days of results</p>\n<p>Control: Results filed in the Vault</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Current rules; minimum-requirement checks; self-assessments; SUS-F06; submissions and receipts; results; CAPA entries.\n\n### SOP 10-06 \\| Innovation and Good-Practice Pipeline\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with Research and Planning. **Purpose:** Try new ideas safely, evaluate them, and adopt only what works.\n\n**Table 10.16**\n\n**SOP 10-06 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                                                                    |\n|-----------------------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-06 (proposed)                                                                             |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | CDRRMO Head approves pilots within existing authority; CDRRMC or SP for pilots needing funds or issuances    |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Research and Planning                                                                                 |\n| Trigger                     | A proposal from staff, a partner or a peer exchange; an AAR or IAT gap that existing methods have not closed |\n| Forms                       | SUS-F07                                                                                                      |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | A10.9 (2, Low, Chapter 7); A10.7 (3, Low)                                                                    |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-11; MR-18                                                                                                 |\n| Policy basis                | RA 10121; RA 10173; UNDRR Scorecard                                                                          |\n| Cross-references            | Chapter 7 (SOPs 7-08, 7-09); Chapter 8 (partners)                                                            |\n\n**Table 10.17**\n\n**SOP 10-06 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Propose</th>\n<th>Proposer</th>\n<th><p>State the problem, the idea, the measure of success, the time limit, the cost and any personal data involved.</p>\n<p>Decision: Linked to a real gap?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Gap source</p>\n<p>Out: SUS-F07 proposal</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Any time</p>\n<p>Control: Data-protection check with the DPO</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Approve the pilot</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Approve, refine or decline; name an owner.</p>\n<p>Decision: Within authority and budget?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Proposal</p>\n<p>Out: Decision</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days</p>\n<p>Control: Decision recorded</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Pilot</th>\n<th>Pilot owner</th>\n<th><p>Run the pilot within its limits; record results.</p>\n<p>Decision: Within time and scope?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Decision</p>\n<p>Out: Pilot records</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: As approved</p>\n<p>Control: No operational reliance during the pilot</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Evaluate</th>\n<th>M&amp;E unit</th>\n<th><p>Evaluate against the success measure.</p>\n<p>Decision: Did it work?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Records</p>\n<p>Out: Evaluation</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: At the time limit</p>\n<p>Control: Method stated</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Adopt, scale or drop</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head</th>\n<th><p>Adopt into the Manual (SOP 7-08), scale, or drop; share the lesson.</p>\n<p>Decision: Needs an issuance or budget? Refer to the CDRRMC.</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Evaluation</p>\n<p>Out: Decision; Manual change; ME-F08 entry</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Within 30 days</p>\n<p>Control: Recorded in the lessons register</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMC</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** SUS-F07 proposals; decisions; pilot records; evaluations; adoption decisions; lessons entries.\n\n### SOP 10-07 \\| Stewardship and Handover of the Manual and MODE\n**Process owner:** CDRRMO Head, with the City ICT office and the platform developer during the engagement. **Purpose:** Leave the City in full control of the Manual and MODE, with no dependence on the developer.\n\n**Table 10.18**\n\n**SOP 10-07 Control Block**\n\n| **Control Element**         | **Entry**                                                              |\n|-----------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Type and MODE template      | Workflow: WF-SOP10-07 (proposed); DEPLOY.md section 8 (turnover)       |\n| Decision authority (legal)  | City Mayor accepts the handover; CDRRMO Head approves Manual versions  |\n| Process owner (operational) | CDRRMO Head; City ICT office                                           |\n| Trigger                     | End of the engagement; change of the City custodian; change of hosting |\n| Forms                       | Annex 10-D; handover record                                            |\n| IAT v1.7 indicators         | B7.1 (Chapter 4 and whole Manual); B3.5 (1, Low)                       |\n| Functional MR links         | MR-01; MR-10; MR-15                                                    |\n| Policy basis                | Engagement contract; RA 10173; RA 9470; ADR-002                        |\n| Cross-references            | Chapters 1 and 7; SOP 10-03                                            |\n\n**Table 10.19**\n\n**SOP 10-07 Steps and Controls**\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style="width: 12%" />\n<col style="width: 15%" />\n<col style="width: 29%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n<col style="width: 21%" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr class="header">\n<th><strong>Step</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Actor</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Action and Decision Rule</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Inputs and Outputs / Records</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Timing, Control and Escalation</strong></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>1. Inventory</th>\n<th>CDRRMO Head with City ICT office</th>\n<th><p>List what must be handed over: approved Manual and source files, Library versions, repository, hosting accounts and keys, runbooks, training materials, registers.</p>\n<p>Decision: Complete?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Annex 10-D</p>\n<p>Out: Handover inventory</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Four weeks before</p>\n<p>Control: Owner office named per item</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Administrator</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>2. Transfer</th>\n<th>Developer; City ICT office</th>\n<th><p>Transfer ownership and credentials; confirm the City holds every account and key.</p>\n<p>Decision: City can sign in to every account without the developer?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Inventory</p>\n<p>Out: Transfer record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before the end date</p>\n<p>Control: Tested by City staff</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>3. Train the custodians</th>\n<th>Developer</th>\n<th><p>Train the City custodian and a local IT contractor on runbooks, migrations and restore.</p>\n<p>Decision: Custodians can run a restore alone?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Runbooks</p>\n<p>Out: Training record; restore by City staff</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: Before the end date</p>\n<p>Control: Observed restore test</p>\n<p>Escalate: CDRRMO Head</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="header">\n<th>4. Remove developer access</th>\n<th>City ICT office</th>\n<th><p>Run the developer-access removal script; confirm in the handover record.</p>\n<p>Decision: Access removed everywhere?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Admin script</p>\n<p>Out: Access removal record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: On the end date</p>\n<p>Control: Second person checks</p>\n<p>Escalate: City Mayor</p></th>\n</tr>\n<tr class="odd">\n<th>5. Accept</th>\n<th>City Mayor</th>\n<th><p>Sign the handover record.</p>\n<p>Decision: All items checked?</p></th>\n<th><p>In: Records</p>\n<p>Out: Signed handover record</p></th>\n<th><p>Time: End of the engagement</p>\n<p>Control: Filed in the Vault</p>\n<p>Escalate: —</p></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n**Minimum records:** Handover inventory; transfer and training records; access removal record; signed handover record.\n\n## 12. Responsibility Assignment (RACI)\n\n| **\\[R\\] Responsible** | **\\[A\\] Accountable** | **\\[C\\] Consulted** | **\\[I\\] Informed** |\n|-----------------------|-----------------------|---------------------|--------------------|\n\n"A" is the single proposed process-accountable owner; "R" performs; "C" is consulted; "I" is informed. The RACI allocates work; it does not transfer legal powers.\n\n**Table 10.20**\n\n*RACI for Sustainability, Recognition and Deep Resilience*\n\n| **Activity**                             | **MY** | **SP** | **CC** | **HD** | **RP** | **AT** | **CA** | **HR** | **ICT** | **DPO** | **LG** |\n|------------------------------------------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|---------|---------|--------|\n| Annual sustainability review and roadmap | I      | I      | A      | R      | R      | C      | C      | C      | C       | –       | –      |\n| Legal permanence instruments             | A      | A      | C      | R      | R      | C      | C      | C      | –       | –       | R      |\n| Leadership transition and succession     | A      | I      | I      | R      | C      | R      | R      | R      | C       | –       | C      |\n| Digital continuity and security          | A      | –      | I      | C      | –      | C      | C      | –      | R       | R       | C      |\n| Annual continuity check                  | I      | –      | I      | A      | C      | R      | C      | C      | R       | –       | –      |\n| Gawad KALASAG and SGLG                   | A      | I      | C      | R      | R      | C      | I      | –      | –       | –       | –      |\n| Innovation pipeline                      | I      | –      | C      | A      | R      | C      | –      | –      | C       | C       | –      |\n| Stewardship and handover                 | A      | –      | I      | R      | C      | C      | C      | –      | R       | C       | C      |\n\n*Note. MY = City Mayor; SP = Sangguniang Panlungsod; CC = CDRRMC; HD = CDRRMO Head; RP = CDRRMO Research and Planning; AT = CDRRMO Administration and Training; CA = City Administrator; HR = City HRMO; ICT = City ICT office; DPO = City Data Protection Officer; LG = City Legal Office. For legal permanence the SP enacts ordinances and the Mayor issues executive orders.*\n\n## 13. Performance Monitoring, Quality Assurance and Corrective Action\n\n**Table 10.21**\n\n*Performance Measures for Chapter 10*\n\n| **Measure**                                                                  | **Evidence**    | **Review Owner**   |\n|------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------|--------------------|\n| Legal instruments enacted or issued as planned (ordinance amendment, EOC EO) | SUS-F01         | CDRRMC             |\n| Critical roles with an alternate and a current succession order              | SUS-F03         | CDRRMO Head        |\n| Transition briefing held within 30 days of new leadership                    | Briefing record | City Administrator |\n| Restore tests passed; access reviews done; backups present                   | SUS-F04         | City ICT office    |\n| Alternate EOC operational within the target time in the annual drill         | Drill record    | CDRRMO Head        |\n| PSCP exercised; essential functions continued                                | Exercise record | City Administrator |\n| Recognition submissions complete and on time; gaps entered and closed        | SUS-F06; ME-F05 | CDRRMC             |\n| IMI, Mandatory compliance and Interface Index against CDRRMC targets         | IAT results     | CDRRMC             |\n| Pilots evaluated before adoption                                             | SUS-F07         | CDRRMO Head        |\n\nFindings go to the CAPA register under Chapter 7 (SOP 7-05).\n\n## 14. Implementation, Validation and Institutionalisation Priorities\n\n**Table 10.22**\n\n*Implementation Priorities for Chapter 10*\n\n| **No.** | **Action**                                                                                                                                 | **Office**                              | **IAT**      | **When**                       |\n|---------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------|--------------|--------------------------------|\n| 1       | Submit the amendment of Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014 (Annex 1-A) and the EOC establishment EO (Annex 1-C).                                   | CDRRMO; City Legal Office; SP; Mayor    | A1.1, B1.1   | First quarter 2027             |\n| 2       | Designate the alternate EOC and hold the first relocation drill.                                                                           | CDRRMO Head; EOC Manager                | B2.7 (High)  | Before the 2027 typhoon season |\n| 3       | Request creation and funding of the planned plantilla items in the FY 2028 budget.                                                         | CDRRMO Head; HRMO; Budget Office        | A2.3 (High)  | FY 2028 budget call            |\n| 4       | Approve the written succession order and prepare the transition pack.                                                                      | CDRRMO Head; City Administrator         | A2.2         | First quarter 2027             |\n| 5       | Decide production hosting for MODE; run the first restore test; adopt the EOC information-security policy; arrange a second internet path. | City Mayor; City ICT office; DPO        | B3.3, B3.5   | Before operational use         |\n| 6       | Complete the PSCP and run the first continuity check with the EOC Capability Assessment Checklist.                                         | CDRRMO; PSCP team                       | A3.4, B7.6   | 2027                           |\n| 7       | File the Gawad KALASAG CY 2026 results and assessor comments; enter the gaps in the CAPA register.                                         | Research and Planning                   | A10.7        | On release of results          |\n| 8       | Update the MODE Gawad KALASAG self-check to the CY 2026 tiers and criteria weights.                                                        | Platform data custodian                 | A10.7        | Next release                   |\n| 9       | Hold the first annual sustainability review and approve the roadmap.                                                                       | CDRRMO; CDRRMC                          | All          | First quarter 2027             |\n| 10      | Complete the stewardship handover at the end of the engagement.                                                                            | CDRRMO Head; City ICT office; developer | MR-01, MR-10 | End of the engagement          |\n\nValidation: SOPs 10-02 and 10-03 are reviewed by the City Administrator, HRMO, ICT office, DPO and City Legal Office; SOP 10-04 is tested in the first continuity check; SOP 10-07 is confirmed by a restore performed by City staff alone.\n\n## 15. Forms, Registers and Annexes\n\n**Table 10.23**\n\n*Chapter 10 Forms and Registers*\n\n| **Code** | **Form or Register**                           | **Used in** | **MODE**                     |\n|----------|------------------------------------------------|-------------|------------------------------|\n| SUS-F01  | Legal instrument tracker                       | SOP 10-01   | Proposed; Vault              |\n| SUS-F02  | Annual sustainability checklist (five pillars) | SOP 10-01   | Proposed                     |\n| SUS-F03  | Succession order and alternates                | SOP 10-02   | Proposed; EOC designations   |\n| SUS-F04  | Backup, restore and access review log          | SOP 10-03   | Proposed                     |\n| SUS-F05  | Annual continuity check (both EOC sites)       | SOP 10-04   | Proposed                     |\n| SUS-F06  | Recognition submission and results tracker     | SOP 10-05   | Proposed; GK self-check tool |\n| SUS-F07  | Innovation pilot proposal and evaluation       | SOP 10-06   | Proposed                     |\n\nAnnexes: **10-A** IAT and master requirements traceability; **10-B** Gawad KALASAG criteria and IAT crosswalk; **10-C** leadership transition pack contents; **10-D** stewardship and handover checklist.\n\n## 16. Relationship With Other Chapters\n\n**Table 10.24**\n\n*Links Between Chapter 10 and the Other Chapters*\n\n| **Ch.** | **Title**                                                                 | **Link**                                                                                                  |\n|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| 1       | Legal Mandate, DRRM Governance and Institutional Accountability           | Owns the instruments whose status SOP 10-01 tracks; its PDCA and CAPA design underlies the whole chapter. |\n| 2       | CDRRMO Organisational Structure, Staffing and HR Management               | Owns plantilla, succession and the EOC facility and alternate EOC that SOPs 10-02 and 10-04 test.         |\n| 3       | Risk Intelligence, Planning and Institutional Preparedness                | Owns the PSCP and the LDRRMP that the roadmap follows.                                                    |\n| 4       | Early Warning, EOC Activation, Incident Management and Emergency Response | Operates the EOC whose continuity is tested here.                                                         |\n| 5       | Recovery, Rehabilitation and Build Back Better                            | Contributes recovery outcomes to deep resilience.                                                         |\n| 6       | DRRM Financing, Resource Management and Accountability                    | Holds the budget lines that keep the system running.                                                      |\n| 7       | Monitoring, Evaluation, Knowledge Management and Continuous Improvement   | Measures progress; holds the CAPA register, lessons and Manual revision.                                  |\n| 8       | Stakeholder Engagement and Institutional Partnerships                     | Keeps the partnerships and networks that support deeper resilience.                                       |\n| 9       | Public Awareness, Community Resilience and Capacity Development           | Builds the capacity that must outlast terms of office.                                                    |\n\n## 17. Concluding Notes for Chapter 10 and the Manual\n\nOrmoc has learned at great cost what disasters do: the 1991 flash flood, Yolanda, the 2017 earthquake and the typhoons since. It has built a Council, an Office, a fund, plans, partnerships and a record of helping others. This Manual gathers those pieces into one system and adds the controls that let the City see whether the system works and fix it when it does not.\n\nThe test of the Manual is not its approval but its use: a warning turned into action in time, an anticipatory action funded before impact, a family that finds a safe evacuation centre, a barangay that counts its people within hours, a recovery that builds back safer, a lesson that changes a procedure. This last chapter asks the City to protect that system from the slow forces that erode institutions, and to keep raising its standard year after year.\n\nWriting these procedures does not make the system last. Until the CDRRMO''s full legal basis is enacted, the alternate EOC has been drilled, MODE runs on City-owned hosting with a tested restore, the first sustainability review has been held and the stewardship handover is complete, this chapter remains a controlled working draft.\n\n## References\n\nThe official text governs.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2023). *Ormoc City disaster risk reduction and management ten-year plan (2023–2032)*.\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026a). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC institutional assessment tool* (Version 1.7) \\[Unpublished assessment workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026b). *Ormoc CDRRMO–EOC master requirements traceability and 140-indicator data dictionary* (Version 1.2) \\[Unpublished project reference workbook\\].\n\nCity Government of Ormoc. (2026c). *MODE architecture decision records ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-014, ADR-021 and ADR-026; DEPLOY.md; runbook-backup-restore.md* \\[Repository documents, version 1.16.0\\]. https://github.com/asilvainnovations/ormoc-drrmo\n\nCity Government of Ormoc, Sangguniang Panlungsod. (2014–2015). *Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014, as amended by Ordinance No. 011, s. 2015*.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2018). *Memorandum No. 131, s. 2018: Guidelines on the establishment, operationalisation and management of the emergency operations center*.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council. (2025). *Memorandum Circular No. 03, s. 2025* (Gawad KALASAG guidelines), 11 April 2025.\n\nNDRRMC, Department of Budget and Management, Department of the Interior and Local Government, & Civil Service Commission. (2014). *Joint Memorandum Circular No. 2014-1*.\n\nNational Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Council & Office of Civil Defence. (n.d.). *Public service continuity planning guidebook*.\n\nNational Privacy Commission. (2016). *Circular No. 16-03: Personal data breach management*.\n\nNational Privacy Commission. (2023). *Circular No. 2023-06: Security of personal data in the government and the private sector*.\n\nOffice of Civil Defence. (2017). *Emergency operations center capability assessment checklist*.\n\nOffice of Civil Defence. (2026). *Memorandum No. 763, s. 2026: Guidelines for the conduct of the Gawad KALASAG Seal for local DRRM councils and offices CY 2026* (corrected).\n\nRepublic Act No. 9470. (2007). *National Archives of the Philippines Act of 2007*.\n\nRepublic Act No. 10121. (2010). *Philippine Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Act of 2010*.\n\nRepublic Act No. 10173. (2012). *Data Privacy Act of 2012*.\n\nRepublic Act No. 11292. (2019). *Seal of Good Local Governance Act of 2019*.\n\nUnited Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2015). *Sendai framework for disaster risk reduction 2015–2030*.\n\nUnited Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction. (2017). *Disaster resilience scorecard for cities: Detailed level assessment*.\n\n## Annex 10-A. IAT and Master Requirements Traceability\n\n**Table 10.25**\n\n*IAT Rows, Master Requirements and Chapter 10 Procedures*\n\n| **IAT** | **Type** | **Weight (W)** | **Score** | **Gap Priority** | **Master Requirements** | **SOP** | **Recommended Action (IAT v1.7)**                                                                                                     |\n|---------|----------|----------------|-----------|------------------|-------------------------|---------|---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n| A1.1    | M        | 3              | 2         | 9.0 High         | MR-02                   | 10-01   | Provide the CDRRMO with its JMC 2014-1 divisions and functions by ordinance (amend Ordinance No. 008, s. 2014; Chapter 1, Annex 1-A). |\n| A2.2    | M        | 3              | 3         | 4.5 Medium       | MR-02                   | 10-02   | Maintain; document succession (OIC order of precedence).                                                                              |\n| A2.3    | M        | 3              | 2         | 9.0 High         | MR-02                   | 10-01   | Compute filled/required positions; request creation and funding of the planned items in the FY 2028 budget.                           |\n','Loaded from reference/moo/consolidated/10_Chapter_10.md (split from MOO_Consolidated_v1.0.md, 12 Nov 2026)','draft')
